`DecodeDatumNative` is **NOT** OpenCV-free! When `datum->encoded()` is true, it calls `DecodeDatumToCVMatNative` (which uses cv::imdecode) and `CVMatToDatum` (which uses cv::Mat). So it DOES depend on OpenCV when the datum is encoded.

However, in OUR case, the CIFAR-10 LMDB data is stored as RAW `datum.data()` (CHW bytes, `encoded=false`), NOT as encoded image strings. So `datum->encoded()` will be FALSE for all our data, and `DecodeDatumNative` will just return false without touching OpenCV at runtime. But at COMPILE time, it still references cv::Mat, cv::imdecode, etc., so it can't compile without OpenCV headers... wait, it's currently inside #ifdef USE_OPENCV so it doesn't compile without OpenCV. If I move it outside the guard, it would need cv::Mat, cv::imdecode which require OpenCV headers — which we don't have included (USE_OPENCV=0 means the cv includes at top are excluded).

So moving DecodeDatumNative outside the #ifdef USE_OPENCV would fail to compile (no cv::Mat). 

